**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), the whole boundary/evolution sections in chapter 10, linked with chapters 3–4 and 7; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), orogenesis and tectonic history; [Introduction to Geology — Perron and Jagoutz, MIT 12.001](../sources/MIT-Introduction-to-Geology.lingua#r_423FFVY46CGEN2F2263F95KX6N), complete relevant tectonic lectures.

Compare divergent, convergent, and transform settings by their relative motions and physical processes. At a ridge, extension, melting, cooling, hydrothermal circulation, and new lithosphere connect different evidence. At a subduction zone, the descending slab, overriding plate, fluids, melting, seismicity, and deformation require a geometry and history rather than a single boundary symbol.

Continental collision can thicken and deform crust through several processes; subsequent extension or erosion can expose an earlier deep record. A transform accommodates relative sliding, but bends, steps, and broader boundary zones may add local compression or extension. Real regions often preserve changes between settings.

Use field relationships, structures, ages, metamorphic conditions, sedimentary histories, and geophysical evidence together. A volcanic composition or a fold alone is not a unique tectonic diagnosis. Distinguish the observed association from the proposed process and test alternatives against the regional history.

**Work:** compare a ridge, subduction/collision system, and transform using complete textbook/course treatments. Draw material and heat pathways and explain what evidence constrains each. For one ancient region, propose two settings compatible with part of the evidence and identify a discriminating observation or age relationship.

Return to rock formation and time when a reconstruction depends on them. The infrastructure connection comes later through geological processes concentrating materials and shaping ground/water conditions, rather than treating every tectonic description as an immediately usable resource prediction.

**Continue:** [Connect volcanic products with magma, transport, eruption, and later change](Volcanic-systems-products-and-evidence.lingua#r_4PFCEXS09TEDAV09B9YMYG8YV4).

